{"id":"opensearch-service","corpus":"aws","title":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Amazon OpenSearch Service"},{"kind":"p","text":"Amazon OpenSearch Service is a managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters in the AWS Cloud. An OpenSearch Service domain is synonymous with an OpenSearch cluster. Domains are clusters with the settings, instance types, instance counts, and storage resources that you specify. Amazon OpenSearch Service supports OpenSearch and legacy Elasticsearch OSS (up to 7.10, the final open source version of the software). When you create a domain, you have the option of which search engine to use."},{"kind":"p","text":"OpenSearch is a fully open-source search and analytics engine for use cases such as log analytics, real-time application monitoring, and clickstream analysis. For more information, see the OpenSearch documentation."},{"kind":"p","text":"Amazon OpenSearch Service provisions all the resources for your OpenSearch cluster and launches it. It also automatically detects and replaces failed OpenSearch Service nodes, reducing the overhead associated with self-managed infrastructures. You can scale your cluster with a single API call or a few clicks in the console."},{"kind":"p","text":"To get started using OpenSearch Service, you create an OpenSearch Service domain, which is equivalent to an OpenSearch cluster. Each EC2 instance in the cluster acts as one OpenSearch Service node."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use the OpenSearch Service console to set up and configure a domain in minutes. If you prefer programmatic access, you can use the AWS CLI, the AWS SDKs, or Terraform."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Features of Amazon OpenSearch Service"},{"kind":"p","text":"OpenSearch Service includes the following features:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Scale"},{"kind":"li","text":"Numerous configurations of CPU, memory, and storage capacity known as instance types, including cost-effective Graviton instances"},{"kind":"li","text":"Supports up to 1002 data nodes"},{"kind":"li","text":"Up to 25 PB of attached storage"},{"kind":"li","text":"Cost-effective UltraWarm and cold storage for read-only data"},{"kind":"p","text":"Security"},{"kind":"li","text":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) access control"},{"kind":"li","text":"Easy integration with Amazon VPC and VPC security groups"},{"kind":"li","text":"Encryption of data at rest and node-to-node encryption"},{"kind":"li","text":"Amazon Cognito, HTTP basic, or SAML authentication for OpenSearch Dashboards"},{"kind":"li","text":"Index-level, document-level, and field-level security"},{"kind":"li","text":"Audit logs"},{"kind":"li","text":"Dashboards multi-tenancy"},{"kind":"p","text":"Stability"},{"kind":"li","text":"Numerous geographical locations for your resources, known as Regions and Availability Zones"},{"kind":"li","text":"Node allocation across two or three Availability Zones in the same AWS Region, known as Multi-AZ"},{"kind":"li","text":"Dedicated master nodes to offload cluster management tasks"},{"kind":"li","text":"Automated snapshots to back up and restore OpenSearch Service domains"},{"kind":"p","text":"Flexibility"},{"kind":"li","text":"SQL support for integration with business intelligence (BI) applications"},{"kind":"li","text":"Custom packages to improve search results"},{"kind":"p","text":"Integration with popular services"},{"kind":"li","text":"Data visualization using OpenSearch Dashboards"},{"kind":"li","text":"Integration with Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring OpenSearch Service domain metrics and setting alarms"},{"kind":"li","text":"Integration with AWS CloudTrail for auditing configuration API calls to OpenSearch Service domains"},{"kind":"li","text":"Integration with Amazon S3, Amazon Kinesis, and Amazon DynamoDB for loading streaming data into OpenSearch Service"},{"kind":"li","text":"Alerts from Amazon SNS when your data exceeds certain thresholds"},{"kind":"h2","text":"When to use OpenSearch versus Amazon OpenSearch Service"},{"kind":"p","text":"Use the following table to help you decide whether provisioned Amazon OpenSearch Service or self-managed OpenSearch is the correct choice for you."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Supported versions of Elasticsearch and OpenSearch"},{"kind":"p","text":"OpenSearch Service supports the following versions of OpenSearch:"},{"kind":"li","text":"3.5, 3.3, 3.1, 2.19, 2.17, 2.15, 2.13, 2.11, 2.9, 2.7, 2.5, 2.3, 1.3, 1.2, 1.1, and 1.0"},{"kind":"p","text":"OpenSearch Service supports the following versions of legacy Elasticsearch:"},{"kind":"li","text":"7.10, 7.9, 7.8, 7.7, 7.4, 7.1, 6.8, 6.7, 6.5, 6.4, 6.3, 6.2, 6.0, 5.6, 5.5, 5.3, 5.1, 2.3, and 1.5"},{"kind":"p","text":"We recommend upgrading to the latest available OpenSearch version to get the best use of OpenSearch Service, in terms of price-performance, feature richness, and security improvements. For information about upgrade paths, breaking changes, and version-specific considerations, see Upgrading Amazon OpenSearch Service domains."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Standard and extended support"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS provides bug fixes and security updates for versions under standard support. For versions in extended support, AWS offers critical security fixes for at least 12 months after standard support ends. Extended support charges apply automatically when a domain runs a version that is no longer under standard support. To avoid these charges, upgrade to a supported version. For running version on Extended Support, you will either be charged, equal to your instance costs (storage costs are not affected), or a flat fee per Normalized Instance Hour (NIH), in addition to the standard instance cost. NIH is computed as a factor of the instance size (e.g. medium, large), and number of instance hours. For versions where the originally published extended support date has passed, and there has been an additional extension to the end of support date, extended support fee will be equal to your instance cost (in addition to the instance cost) and storage costs are not affected. See Calculating extended support charges below for details and examples."}],"lemmas":{"amazons":["amazon","amazons"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"manageds":["manageds","managed"],"manages":["manages","manage"],"managing":["managing","manage"],"makes":["make","makes"],"making":["making","make"],"made":["made","make"],"easies":["easies","easy"],"easier":["easier","easy"],"deploys":["deploys","deploy"],"deployed":["deployed","deploy"],"deploying":["deploying","deploy"],"operates":["operates","operate"],"operated":["operated","operate"],"operating":["operating","operate"],"scales":["scale","scales"],"scaling":["scaling","scale"],"scaled":["scaled","scale"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"clouds":["cloud","clouds"],"clouding":["clouding","cloud"],"clouded":["clouded","cloud"],"domains":["domain","domains"],"settings":["setting","settings"],"types":["type","types"],"typed":["typed","type"],"typing":["typing","type"],"counts":["count","counts"],"counting":["counting","count"],"counted":["counted","count"],"storages":["storage","storages"],"resources":["resource","resources"],"specifies":["specifies","specify"],"specified":["specified","specify"],"specifying":["specifying","specify"],"supports":["support","supports"],"supporting":["supporting","support"],"legacies":["legacy","legacies"],"elasticsearches":["elasticsearches","elasticsearch"],"finals":["final","finals"],"opens":["open","opens"],"opened":["opened","open"],"opening":["opening","open"],"sources":["source","sources"],"sourcing":["sourcing","source"],"whens":["whens","when"],"creates":["creates","create"],"created